SB-Prolog 2.2 on OS-9/68K  --  how to start it==============================================A full Prolog: interpreter, compiler, module library and the Stony Brookdocumentation.  Copyright SUNY at Stony Brook 1986, GPL -- see/dd/SBPROLOG/COPYING, which must travel with any copy you pass on.THE ONE THING THAT WILL WASTE YOUR AFTERNOON  wam.sbprolog needs the environment variable SIMPATH pointing at the module  library.  Without it the system starts, loads the byte-code file, runs, and  exits with      Halt. Program terminated normally  and NO banner and NO prompt.  That looks exactly like a broken build.  It is  not.  It is $init_sys calling $getenv(SIMPATH) and getting nothing.STARTING IT      setenv SIMPATH /dd/SBPROLOG/MODLIB      wam.sbprolog /dd/SBPROLOG/MODLIB/$readloop  You should get:      SB-Prolog Version 2.2      | ?-  Then, for example:      | ?- X is 3+4, write(X), nl.  halt.  leaves Prolog.  Feeding queries from a file works too:      wam.sbprolog /dd/SBPROLOG/MODLIB/$readloop <myqueries  End of input is a normal exit, not an error.WHAT IS WHERE  /dd/CMDS/wam.sbprolog   the WAM emulator -- the whole runtime  /dd/SBPROLOG/MODLIB/    the byte-code modules, and MODLIB_SRC/ their .P source  /dd/SBPROLOG/CMPLIB/    the Prolog-to-byte-code compiler  /dd/SBPROLOG/LIB/       library predicates ($exists, statistics, setof, ...)  /dd/SBPROLOG/PROG/      example programs and BENCHMARKS/  /dd/DOC/sbprolog/       sbprolog.doc -- the full Stony Brook manual, 183K  /dd/SRC/sbprolog/       the C source of the emulator, with its OS-9 makefiles  Not everything is loaded at startup.  append/3, for one, answers `no' until  you consult the list library.  That is SB-Prolog behaving normally.USEFUL FLAGS  (wam.sbprolog with no arguments prints the full list)  -m <words>   local+heap stack size, default 100000 words of 4 bytes  -p <words>   program area, default 100000  -T           trace entry to each called routine -- this is how the SIMPATH               requirement was found; it is worth knowing about  -d           dump the byte-code file to dump.pil instead of running itHOW THIS BINARY WAS BUILT  From /dd/SRC/sbprolog, following the tree's own OS-9 makefiles: the 15  builtin sources compiled with -DOS9 and merged into builtin.l, then the 14  simulator sources linked against it.  Trap-free -- no cio.  The archive also shipped a prebuilt wam.sbprolog (71158 bytes).  Ours is  72088.  Both were run side by side on the same queries and behave  identically; ours is the one installed, because its provenance is the source  beside it.  The sources already carried #ifdef OS9 -- somebody did this port  properly, long ago, and left the makefiles to prove it.